 #### Resolution details and query consistency
 
 - The library receives explicit environment values, not ambient environment access. The daemon must capture them once at startup (C3 wiring). Each supplied environment string is at most 64 KiB and contains no ASCII control characters. Data and PATH locations are absolute, lexically normalized UTF-8 paths of at most 4096 bytes; empty/unset XDG data values select the documented defaults. An empty/unset PATH is an empty search list; empty or relative components in a nonempty path list fail startup validation instead of searching the daemon's working directory. Desktop tokens are exact colon-separated values; empty tokens are ignored.
 - Installed traversal is byte-sorted depth-first, with the applications directory at nesting depth 0. All enumerated entries, including ignored entries and parent siblings, reserve the shared traversal budget before recursion. If distinct relative paths in one root flatten to the same desktop-entry ID, the first encountered path wins deterministically. Lower-priority masked files are not parsed; a malformed selected winner fails the query rather than exposing a lower entry. Symlinks in installed roots or their ancestors are skipped, as are symlink/non-regular directory entries. A present non-directory root or inaccessible real directory fails with `filesystem.unavailable`; missing roots are empty.
-- The desktop-entry parser accepts LF/CRLF with an optional final newline, not bare CR separators or a BOM. It validates duplicate groups/keys and general string escapes (`\s`, `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`), including ignored localized/vendor/action data. Recognized string-list keys additionally accept escaped semicolons. Standard boolean keys accept only `true`/`false`; `OnlyShowIn` and `NotShowIn` are mutually exclusive. `Encoding`, if present, must be `UTF-8`. Validation precedes visibility filtering, including for hidden entries. This is a bounded discovery parser, not a launcher or exhaustive linter for every desktop-entry extension: Exec text is returned after general string-unescaping, without shell parsing or field-code expansion.
